<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<cfsSimulation xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
               xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation ../../../../../share/xml/CFS-Simulation/CFS.xsd"
               xmlns="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation">

  <documentation>
    <title>Oblique frictional impact of an organic, unstructured tet-meshed body on a slab</title>
    <authors><author>kroppert</author></authors>
    <date>2026-08-08</date>
    <keywords><keyword>contact</keyword></keywords>
    <references>none</references>
    <isVerified>no</isVerified>
    <description>
      An ORGANICALLY SHAPED body -- lumpy, non-primitive curvature, no symmetry, no analytic
      radius -- meshed UNSTRUCTURED with TET4, strikes a free deformable slab obliquely with
      Coulomb friction under large sliding. Nothing is fixed anywhere: both bodies are free, the
      only force in the model is the contact traction, and the striker slides while the contact
      patch forms, grows and travels.

      This is ContactImpactOblique3d with the cube-sphere striker replaced by an organic one.
      Its contact facets are TRIA3 against the slab's QUAD9, which also makes it the first MIXED
      contact pair in the suite (a tet body meeting a hex body).

      INVARIANTS. 1. LINEAR MOMENTUM. Both bodies are free and the contact tractions are equal
      and opposite, so total linear momentum is conserved. This holds for the DISCRETE system by
      construction, independently of mesh, shape, facet quality and contact resolution -- which
      makes it the right primary invariant for a body nobody can write an exact answer for. 2.
      COULOMB. sqrt(Fx^2 + Fy^2) / Fz = mu while every point slips, to the extent the primary
      stays flat. 3. SYMMETRY is deliberately NOT available: an organic body has none to assert.
      That is not a regression, it is the definition of the goal -- and it retires the tie-break
      hazard that cost ContactImpactOblique3d orders of magnitude of its symmetry invariant when
      a facet boundary landed on its mirror plane.
    </description>
  </documentation>

  <fileFormats>
    <input>
      <hdf5 fileName="ContactImpactOrganic3d.h5" linearizeEntities="__none__"/>
    </input>
    <output><hdf5/></output>
    <materialData file="mat.xml" format="xml"/>
  </fileFormats>

  <domain geometryType="3d">
    <regionList>
      <region name="slab" material="Aluminium"/>
      <region name="ball" material="Aluminium"/>
    </regionList>
    <surfRegionList>
      <surfRegion name="slab_top"/>
      <surfRegion name="ball_surf"/>
    </surfRegionList>
  </domain>

  <fePolynomialList>
    <Lagrange><gridOrder/></Lagrange>
  </fePolynomialList>

  <integrationSchemeList>
    <scheme>
      <method>Gauss</method>
      <order>4</order>
      <mode>absolute</mode>
    </scheme>
  </integrationSchemeList>

  <sequenceStep>
    <analysis>
      <transient>
        <numSteps>50</numSteps>
        <deltaT>5e-6</deltaT>
      </transient>
    </analysis>

    <pdeList>
      <mechanic subType="3d">
        <regionList>
          <!-- Only the sphere is launched; the slab is free and at rest, so every bit of its
               motion arrives through the contact. -->
          <region name="slab"/>
          <region name="ball" initialFieldD1Id="launch"/>
        </regionList>

        <contactList>
          <!-- primary = the FLAT slab top: that is what makes the contact normal exactly
               (0,0,1) and the reported force split exact. -->
          <contactPair name="organic_impact"
                       primarySurface="slab_top"
                       secondarySurface="ball_surf"
                       slidingType="large"
                       segmentation="mortar"
                       normalSmoothing="nodal"
                       normalPenalty="100"
                       initialGapHandling="fromMesh"
                       integrationOrder="2" searchDistanceFactor="2">
            <friction type="coulomb" mu="0.2" tangentialPenalty="100" tangent="consistent"/>
          </contactPair>
        </contactList>

        <initialValues>
          <initialFieldD1 id="launch" quantity="mechVelocity">
            <vector>
              <comp dof="x" value="20.0"/>
              <comp dof="z" value="-15.0"/>
            </vector>
          </initialFieldD1>
        </initialValues>

        <!-- Deliberately empty: no load, no support, nothing fixed. -->
        <bcsAndLoads/>

        <storeResults>
          <nodeResult type="mechDisplacement"><allRegions/></nodeResult>
          <nodeResult type="mechVelocity"><allRegions/></nodeResult>
        </storeResults>
      </mechanic>
    </pdeList>

    <linearSystems>
      <system>
        <solutionStrategy>
          <standard>
            <setup idbcHandling="elimination"/>
            <matrix storage="sparseNonSym" reordering="noReordering"/>
            <nonLinear method="newton" logging="no">
              <lineSearch type="none"/>
              <!-- A free impact carries no applied load, so RhsLinL2Norm is zero and
                   residualErr falls back to the ABSOLUTE residual, which no resStopCrit can
                   reach. Judge on incStopCrit; this limitation is shared with every
                   nonlinear PDE. -->
              <incStopCrit>1e-9</incStopCrit>
              <resStopCrit>1e-2</resStopCrit>
              <maxNumIters>50</maxNumIters>
            </nonLinear>
          </standard>
        </solutionStrategy>
        <solverList><directLU/></solverList>
      </system>
    </linearSystems>

  </sequenceStep>
</cfsSimulation>
